1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:24,620
 Research papers are not written to be read from start to finish, like a novel.

2
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:29,780
 So in this video, I will show you the parts of a paper and the order to read them in.

3
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:32,180
 There's this method I like, it's called a three-pass method.

4
00:00:32,180 --> 00:00:35,180
 And that's the method I'm going to be focusing on in this video.

5
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:38,140
 Most research papers are built from the same parts.

6
00:00:38,140 --> 00:00:40,860
 And you need to know what each part is for,

7
00:00:40,860 --> 00:00:44,820
 so that you can stop reading it like one big block of text.

8
00:00:44,820 --> 00:00:48,300
 In a lot of fields, the main body follows a pattern called IMRAD.

9
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:52,340
 It stands for introduction, methods, results, and discussion.

10
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:54,060
 And around those, you usually would

11
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:58,660
 see a title, an abstract, related work, background section,

12
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:02,700
 a limitation section, a conclusion, and the references.

13
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:05,020
 The names will change a little from field to field

14
00:01:05,020 --> 00:01:07,180
 in HCI, which is my field.

15
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:09,700
 The results section is often called findings.

16
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:12,340
 You don't need to read every part of a research paper

17
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:13,100
 closely.

18
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:15,580
 There are three major parts that do most of the work for you.

19
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:17,100
 The first one is the abstract.

20
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:20,380
 The abstract is a short paragraph right under the title.

21
00:01:20,380 --> 00:01:24,100
 It's the old papers squeezed into one paragraph.

22
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:26,940
 What they asked, what they did, what they found,

23
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:28,420
 summarized in the abstract.

24
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:29,860
 The second one is the method.

25
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:31,540
 The method is where the authors tells you

26
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:34,660
 exactly what they did, who took part, how many people,

27
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:36,780
 what they measure, how they measured it.

28
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:38,060
 This is the path that tells you

29
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:40,460
 whether you can trust everything else in the paper.

30
00:01:40,460 --> 00:01:41,860
 And the third path is the discussion

31
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:43,140
 to get out with the limitation.

32
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:45,420
 The discussion is where the authors explain

33
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:47,340
 what their results mean.

34
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:49,380
 And the limitation section is where they say

35
00:01:49,380 --> 00:01:52,620
 what their study cannot tell you.

36
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:55,300
 Like, hey, this study does not provide this,

37
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:56,360
 something like that.

38
00:01:56,360 --> 00:01:58,080
 And I think the limitation is one of the most useful parts

39
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:02,400
 paper when you're writing your thesis. For example, because it shows what is still open and you can

40
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:06,640
 often find your research gap there. One of the best known methods for reading research paper comes

41
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:16,160
 from a two-page paper called "How to Read a Paper." It was written by S. Keshav. In his word,

42
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:21,600
 you should read the paper in up to three passes, and each pass has a different job. The first pass

43
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:25,760
 tells you what the research paper is about and whether you even need this paper at all.

44
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:32,720
 and the second part tells you what the paper found and what evidence it has and the third part is for

45
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:38,960
 the third part is for the papers you need to understand completely. Pass one of Keshev's

46
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:44,320
 three-pass method takes about five to ten minutes and it also gives you the order to read the paper

47
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:50,800
 in. List it up in four steps. First you carefully read the title of the paper, you read the abstract

48
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:55,760
 of the paper, you read the introduction. Then second, you read the section and the

49
00:02:55,760 --> 00:03:00,400
 sub-section heading, but ignore every other thing. Then third, you read the conclusion,

50
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:04,720
 and fourth, you glance over the references. Just manually tick off the ones that you

51
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:08,240
 have already read or you've already seen. If you watch my video on how to read the book,

52
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:12,800
 this is kind of the same idea of reading the structure first, same idea kind of applied to

53
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:17,680
 research papers. At the end of the first pass, according to Keshav's method, you should be able

54
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:21,680
 to answer five questions, and he calls them the five Cs. So when you've done the first

55
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:25,120
 pass of reading a research paper, according to this method, you should be able to answer

56
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:26,040
 these five questions.

57
00:03:26,040 --> 00:03:30,760
 Category is what kind of research paper is this? Is it a study? Is it a new system? Is

58
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:37,000
 it a review of other papers? Number two, context. What other papers is this paper related to?

59
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,240
 Number three, correctness. No. Number three, correctness is whether the assumptions seem

60
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:48,000
 valid. Number four, contribution is what the paper has that was not in this

61
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:53,160
 subject of study before. And clarity is whether the paper is well written. A lot

62
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:57,060
 of time after those five, ten minutes you would find that maybe the paper is not

63
00:03:57,060 --> 00:04:01,260
 relevant to what you're doing. At this point you stop, save yourself an

64
00:04:01,260 --> 00:04:04,560
 old evening. You just do five, ten minutes to save yourself a whole evening. You don't

65
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:08,340
 want to dedicate three hours, four hours, five hours to reading a research paper that is

66
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:13,380
 relevant to your own study. So the first pass is usually enough for papers that are not in your

67
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:18,980
 research area but maybe they might matter to you someday, who knows. Now pass two, the second pass

68
00:04:18,980 --> 00:04:23,620
 of Keshav's method for reading research paper takes up to an hour. After you've gone through the

69
00:04:23,620 --> 00:04:27,780
 first pass in five to ten minutes you decided that this research paper is relevant to you,

70
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:32,180
 then you move on to reading the paper with more care but you still ignore the detail. So you can

71
00:04:32,180 --> 00:04:37,140
 ignore something like a long table of statistics and you come back to that later. While you read

72
00:04:37,140 --> 00:04:42,420
 during the second pass. Keshav suggests writing down the key point or making comments in the

73
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:47,780
 margin. So if you're not, you can print out your research paper, this is something that I do now,

74
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:51,380
 instead of just having it virtual, you could print it out so you can mark it up.

75
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:55,540
 Now this is the part where you look properly at the figures, you look at the, you look at the

76
00:04:55,540 --> 00:05:00,180
 figures, the diagrams or the illustration in the paper, you know, pay some attentions to the graph.

77
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:03,620
 And on the second pass, you mark some of the references that you haven't read that looks

78
00:05:03,620 --> 00:05:09,620
 relevant to you. That's how one good research paper can lead you to another one. So by the

79
00:05:09,620 --> 00:05:14,420
 time you're at the end of the second pass, you should be able to explain the main point of the

80
00:05:14,420 --> 00:05:19,380
 paper and the evidence for it to someone else. Now, if you still don't understand the research

81
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:24,020
 paper at this point, you can put it aside and come back to it later after reading some background,

82
00:05:24,020 --> 00:05:28,820
 or you can move to the third pass. The third pass of Keshev's method for reading research papers

83
00:05:28,820 --> 00:05:32,740
 is for papers that you need to understand completely. The ones that, for example,

84
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:38,740
 your thesis is built on. Kesha writes that the key to the third pass is to attempt to virtually

85
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:43,380
 re-implement the paper. That means that you make the same assumptions as the authors of the research

86
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:48,740
 paper, you review the work in your head, kind of, and then you compare your version with their

87
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:53,860
 version. For a study in HCI that looks something, it looks something like you're imagining, you're

88
00:05:53,860 --> 00:05:58,500
 running the study yourself. Who would you recruit? What would you ask them? Would you measure the same

89
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:03,300
 thing that the authors of this paper measure, you compare that answer with what the authors do.

90
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:09,140
 Every place where your version is different from the version in the paper is either a weak spot in

91
00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:14,660
 the paper or an idea for you for your own research work. And the third part takes time. According to

92
00:06:14,660 --> 00:06:19,380
 the person that started this method, that wrote about this method, it can take about three,

93
00:06:19,380 --> 00:06:23,620
 four, five hours for beginners and about an hour for an experienced reader. I would say

94
00:06:23,620 --> 00:06:29,220
 a student, based on my experience, you should just be on the higher side, just project like 3-4-5 hours.

95
00:06:29,220 --> 00:06:32,420
 But of course you don't deep dive into a paper like that if you haven't first gone through the

96
00:06:32,420 --> 00:06:36,420
 first and the second pass to know that it is actually relevant for your work. Again you don't

97
00:06:36,420 --> 00:06:41,620
 want to commit 5-4 hours to a paper that is not going to help your thesis. Now reading a research

98
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:46,820
 paper critically means checking whether you should believe it because the fact that a paper is

99
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:50,900
 published doesn't also make it the right paper to reference or to build your own research on.

100
00:06:50,900 --> 00:06:53,860
 So there are three things that I would check on every research paper.

101
00:06:53,860 --> 00:06:57,860
 The first is the sample size, which is how many people or things that the authors of

102
00:06:57,860 --> 00:06:58,860
 the paper study.

103
00:06:58,860 --> 00:07:01,340
 Bigger sample size doesn't automatically means better.

104
00:07:01,340 --> 00:07:02,340
 Keep this in mind.

105
00:07:02,340 --> 00:07:05,420
 In HCI, for example, small studies are very common.

106
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:10,820
 Kelly Caine looked at every paper published in 2014 and found that the most common sample

107
00:07:10,820 --> 00:07:12,500
 size was 12.

108
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:17,860
 So 12 people in a long interview can tell you a lot about how people experience something.

109
00:07:17,860 --> 00:07:24,980
 But traffic people can't tell you what percentage of all students do something.

110
00:07:24,980 --> 00:07:28,000
 So the question is whether the number fits the size of the claim.

111
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,880
 The second thing to look out for is the method, which is how they got their answer.

112
00:07:31,880 --> 00:07:33,980
 And confirm whether it matches the question.

113
00:07:33,980 --> 00:07:38,340
 Say a research paper claims that switching between apps stresses people.

114
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:40,560
 Did they have people in a survey?

115
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:42,940
 Did they watch people work?

116
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:46,620
 Did they measure something like time or heart rates?

117
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:47,720
 Was it in a lab?

118
00:07:47,720 --> 00:07:52,180
 it at these people's offices and jobs, who were the participants. If everyone in the

119
00:07:52,180 --> 00:07:57,440
 study was a university student, I'd be very careful applying the results of that study

120
00:07:57,440 --> 00:08:03,280
 to say no-sees on a night shift. The third thing I'd look out for is who funded it. Look

121
00:08:03,280 --> 00:08:08,360
 for a funding section if there is, maybe acknowledgement or a conflict of interest statement. Funding

122
00:08:08,360 --> 00:08:15,840
 doesn't exactly make the study wrong. But in 2017, a cultural review looked at a drug

123
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:22,320
 medical device study and they found that a study sponsored by the companies that made the product,

124
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:27,440
 we are more likely to report results or conclusion that favored that product. So when the funder

125
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:32,080
 benefits from one particular answer, you need to pay a little bit more attention. And coming back

126
00:08:32,080 --> 00:08:36,000
 to the idea of whether printing out your research papers or not, how do you keep notes on your

127
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,200
 research papers? Keeping notes on your research papers matter. How do you even read them? Do you

128
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:44,640
 read them digitally? I have started to print them out, to read them physically, because I think that

129
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:48,400
 works better for me. There are just a few papers that are relevant to my thesis and like they are

130
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:53,200
 really really major for me. So you might want to print those kinds of papers out and just

131
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:57,520
 have them at hand or you can just read them digitally whichever works for you. But keeping

132
00:08:57,520 --> 00:09:01,840
 notes on the research papers that you're using matters because you don't write anything down you

133
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:06,240
 you'll not remember a lot of it. One of the tools that is always recommended in my school is Zotero.

134
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:12,080
 Zotero is a very good research tool and I think it is free and open source. You can open your PDF

135
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:16,720
 inside these highlights, you know, write notes and it and all of that. But after all of this,

136
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:22,320
 if you think that a research paper is relevant to your research topic, but it still doesn't make

137
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:26,640
 sense after following all of the steps, then there are a few things you can do. You can look up some

138
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:30,640
 of the terms that you don't know first of all, because if there are like terms, a lot of terms

139
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:35,440
 in a section that you don't know, it can make the whole section feel impossible to understand.

140
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:39,680
 Or you can find a survey paper on the same topic and read it before you go back to the research

141
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:44,960
 paper. So web paper is kind of like a paper that reviews the research on a topic. Also,

142
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:50,640
 you can use AI to explain like dense paragraphs. I do this a lot. Just check what it tells you

143
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:56,160
 against the paper itself, right? Because they are getting things wrong sometimes. But yeah, that's it.

